= Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer(tm) users =

"""Differences between using Substance 3D Designer and Copernicus."""

== Overview ==

This page is for users that are familiar with Substance 3D Designer(tm) and want to accomplish similar tasks in Houdini using Copernicus (COP). Though this page outlines differences between Substance 3D Designer and Copernicus, both have a dynamic, horizontal network that uses wires to connect nodes.

:platform:Mac
    Use ((Cmd)) instead of ((Ctrl)). See [/basics/macosx] for more macOS specifics.

== Concepts ==

In Houdini, you add, connect, and organize Copernicus nodes in the Network editor. You can then configure a selected node's values in the Parameter editor. Use the [flags|Node:cop/index#flags] on COP nodes to control what's output in the Viewers. There are multiple ways to view your output in the Scene and Composite Viewers, so see [Viewing your output|/copernicus/working_with_cops#viewing-your-output] for more information.

Substance 3D Designer ||
    Copernicus ||

Package |
    A new Houdini instance loads as a .hip file. Instead of a package divided into elements, the [Network editor|/network/nodes] lets you set up multiple subnetworks. Houdini uses [recipes|/network/recipes] and [shelf tools|/shelf/index] instead of package templates.

Nodes |
    Houdini networks are like a computer's file system, where networks are like folders and nodes are like files. Nodes can also be subnetworks that contain other nodes. You can also [reference nodes and their parameters|#parm_ref] in your networks.

Project |
    A [Houdini Digital Asset (HDA)|/assets/intro] is similar to a project made up of .SBS files in Substance 3D Designer.

Explorer panel |
    The network's hierarchy appears above the [Operation toolbar|/basics/ui#opbar] and [Network editor|/network/nodes]. You can also view this information in the [Tree View pane|/ref/panes/treeview].

Main toolbar |
    [Main menus|/ref/menus]

Properties panel |
    [Parameter editor|/network/parms]

    Node-specific parameters appear in the Parameter editor of the node. Values considered __Attributes__ parameters in Substance 3D Designer appear in a node's [info window|/network/info]. Values considered __Base__ parameters in Substance 3D Designer appear in the Parameter editor for only COP Network nodes instead of for each node in the network.

Graph viewport and toolbar |
    [Network editor|/network/nodes] and toolbar

2D and 3D viewports |
    [Scene Viewer|/basics/view] outputs a volume and [Composite Viewer|/ref/views/imgview] outputs an image.

    To display materials in your scene, see [Bringing COPs into LOPs for materials|/copernicus/working_with_cops#bringing-cops-into-lops-for-materials] or [Using COPs as materials|/copernicus/working_with_cops#copmat].

Library panel |
    Use the [Tab menu|/basics/tabmenu] to add [Node:cop/index].

Assets library |
    Install [Houdini Digital Assets|/assets/intro] that Houdini stores in a library file.

Frame |
    [Network box|/network/organize#netbox]

Substance function graphs |
    [VOPs|Node:vop/index] and [/vex/index]

Grayscale |
    Mono

Links |
    Wires

Built-in variables |
    [Variables and attributes|/network/expressions#variables-and-attributes]

== Hotkeys and tips ==

Function ||
    Copernicus ||

Connect nodes |
        * To connect a new node to the output of a selected node, press ((Shift + Enter)) after you find the node in the Tab menu.
        * To connect a node between two nodes, drag the node onto the wire between the two nodes.

Cut wires |
    Hold ((Y)) while you drag the mouse over the wire you want to cut.

    You can also shake a node to disconnect all of its wires.

Swap wires |
    Select the node with the inputs you want to switch and press ((Shift + R)).

Reposition wires |
    Drag a wire to a different input.

Change wire style |
    Press ((Shift + S)) in the network editor to change between straight and rounded wires.

Reset to default values |
    ((RMB)) a parameter and choose __Revert to Defaults__.

Create anchors |
    Hold ((Alt)) and click a wire.

Reframe nodes in the network |
    Press ((F)) in the network editor. The network zooms to frame either the selected node or, if none are selected, all nodes.

Align nodes |
    Press ((L)) in the network editor.

Turn transparency on and off |
    ((RMB)) in the Composite Viewer and turn __Display Options__ on. In the [Display Options toolbar|/ref/views/imgview#display-toolbar], click the __Toggle Transparency__ button on or off. 
        
    You can also press ((Ctrl + Shift + T)) in the Composite Viewer.

Referencing parameters |
    You can copy parameters within the same node or across nodes.
    # ((RMB)) the parameter you want to reference and choose __Copy Parameter__.
    # ((RMB)) the parameter where you want to paste the reference and choose __Paste Relative References__.

    See the following for more information about referencing parameters and nodes:
    * [/network/copying]
    * [Referencing COP networks|/copernicus/working_with_cops#referencing-cop-networks]

    #id: parm_ref

Promoting functions |
    In the Parameter editor of the node you want to edit, click the [Icon:BUTTONS/gear] __Parameter Edit Menu__ button and choose [Edit Parameter Interface|/ref/windows/edit_parameter_interface].

    The Edit Parameter Interface window is similar to the __Parameters tab__ in the Edit Operator Type Properties window, so see [Parameters tab|/ref/windows/optype#parms] for more information.

Inspect pixels |
    ((RMB)) in the Composite Viewer and turn __Inpsect__ on. Hover over the image to view the pixel information.

== Nodes ==

The following table outlines where to find certain Substance 3D Designer node operations in Copernicus. For more information, see [COP nodes|/nodes/cop/index].

NOTE:
    To search for nodes, press ((Tab)) in the Network editor or Viewer.

Substance 3D Designer ||
    Copernicus ||

Alpha Merge node |
    [Node:cop/blend]
    
    Set the __Mode__ to __Add__ and wire an alpha into the `bg` or `fg` input.

Ambient Occlusion (HBAO) node |
    [Node:cop/heighttoambientocclusion]

Auto Levels node |
    [Node:cop/equalize]

Base Material node |
    [Node:cop/previewmaterial]

Bevel node |
    [Node:cop/feather]

Directional Blur node |
    [Node:cop/streakblur]

Distance node |
    [Node:cop/feather] and [Node:cop/extrapolateboundaries]

    See [Distance node|#distance] for more information about how to configure these COPs so they act like a Distance node.

Dot (Portal) node |
    [Node:cop/null] and [Node:cop/fetch]

Flood Fill nodes |
    [Node:cop/segmentbyconnectivity] and [Node:cop/uvmapbyid]

    [Node:cop/randommono] and [Node:cop/randomrgb] nodes perform similar functions as Substance 3D Designer's Flood Fill to Random Grayscale and Flood Fill to Random Color nodes, respectively.

Gradient nodes |
    Ramp nodes

Histogram nodes |
    Remap nodes

    For example, set the __Operation__ to __Threshold__ to perform a similar function as Substance 3D Designer's Histogram Scan node.

